spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:9:The Virtual Office messaging drawer (`MessagingDrawer` → 
`MessageFeed` → `MessageComposer` / `MessageItem`, state in `MessagingContext`, data in `useMessages` / 
`useConversations`, Realtime in `useMessageSubscription` / `useConversationRealtime`, typing in 
`useConversationPresence`) was completed for timeline features in Phase 4. Phase 4 also delivered a failed send that 
keeps text/reply/files with a single-message retry (`clientMessageId` idempotency in `POST /api/messages/create`, 
stored in `messages.client_message_id`), a Realtime catch-up after reconnect, and opt-in desktop notifications for 
DM/group messages. This phase adds: a durable per-user outbox for text messages with server-fenced attempt generations 
and safe cross-tab and cross-account behavior; a connection banner; application-level recovery and health for every 
messaging Realtime stream with polling fallback; a reliable, named, members-only typing indicator; live cross-device 
unread/read sync; @mentions with desktop notifications (including rooms); messaging health metrics as redacted server 
logs; and two Phase 4 database follow-ups plus restoring the open conversation after reload.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:13:Workspace members using the chat on unstable networks or 
sleeping laptops lose work and trust: a send that fails must be retried by hand, a reload or closed tab discards it 
(the idempotency key lives only in component memory), and nothing tells the user the chat is disconnected. Optimistic 
messages are matched to stored ones by guessing from content and time. Realtime recovery is incomplete: the SDK 
retries errored/timed-out channels and the conversation-list stream invalidates the list after its own rejoins, but 
the application exposes health only for the messages channel, does not recover terminal closures of the 
conversation-list channel, has no aggregate health, and has no polling fallback, so new messages and unread counts can 
silently stop updating. The typing indicator disappears after ~4 s of continuous typing and is broadcast on a public 
channel. Reading on one device leaves stale unread badges on the user's other devices. Nobody can mention someone, so 
room conversations (which never notify) cannot reach a specific person. Nobody can tell in production whether 
messaging is healthy.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:30:- With healthy connectivity and Realtime, cross-device/tab sync 
of unread counts, read state, and own sent messages within 5 s (D4); during a Realtime outage the polling deadlines 
above apply.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:54:- Application-level recovery, health, and catch-up for every 
messaging Realtime stream (messages/receipts/reactions, conversation list, typing of the open conversation).
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:55:- Polling fallback after > 30 s of a required stream outage, 
while the tab is visible.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:60:- Phase 4 follow-ups: membership check inside 
`mark_conversation_read`; `get_unread_counts` not executable by `anon`; restore the open conversation after page 
reload.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:87:- J11 Reload restore: A reloads with the drawer open on 
conversation X; the drawer reopens on X.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:96:| FR-004 | Cross-tab ordering and ownership: the outbox order 
is shared by all tabs; per conversation, only the oldest unresolved item is eligible, and at most one tab holds the 
conversation's drain claim at a time (atomic claim). A claim is a lease: it ends when its tab closes or crashes, or 
when its holder exceeds the attempt deadline (request timeout 30 s; lease 45 s), and another tab then resumes after 
fencing (FR-006). When connectivity returns, an attempt that started before or during the outage is superseded 
immediately (fenced and redispatched under a new generation by whichever tab drains), so a hung holder never delays 
the D1 deadline. Across all tabs, no two create requests of the same conversation's outbox are in progress at once. | 
Must | AC-005, AC-041 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:98:| FR-006 | Attempt fencing: each dispatch is an attempt of the 
item's `clientMessageId`. Every create and fence request carries the item's attempt generation (monotonic per key). 
The server accepts a create only if its generation is the current one for that (sender, conversation, key) at the 
moment of commit, and a fence with generation g atomically either returns the stored message (item resolves as 
delivered) or makes every generation below g unable to commit — including requests still in the network that have not 
reached the server; a fence or create with a generation older than the server's current one is rejected. When an 
attempt ends without a usable response (network error, timeout, tab closed, lease expired, superseded, HTTP 5xx), the 
item is unknown; before any cancel, expiry, takeover, or new dispatch, the app fences it. The fence never creates a 
message. | Must | AC-038, AC-042, AC-045 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:106:| FR-014 | Banner: "Sem conexão — mensagens serão enviadas 
quando voltar" as soon as the browser reports offline; "Reconectando…" when any required messaging Realtime stream 
(messages/receipts/reactions, conversation list) has been unhealthy for more than 5 s while the browser is online; it 
disappears by itself once all are healthy. | Must | AC-014 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:107:| FR-015 | Every messaging Realtime stream 
(messages/receipts/reactions, conversation list, typing of the open conversation) has application-level health and 
recovery: after an error, timeout, or terminal closure it rejoins without user action using exponential backoff with a 
capped delay (in addition to the SDK's own retries); on recovery the messages and conversation-list streams trigger a 
catch-up refetch of the open feed and the list; the typing stream rejoins. | Must | AC-015 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:108:| FR-016 | Polling fallback: while any required stream has 
been unhealthy for more than 30 s and the tab is visible, the app fetches the open conversation's new messages, the 
receipt summaries of its loaded messages, any open reader-details list, and the conversation list every 15 s; on 
becoming visible while still unhealthy it fetches immediately, then every 15 s; no polling while hidden; once all 
streams are healthy no new polling request starts. Merging polling and Realtime results never duplicates a message and 
never replaces newer or enriched state (attachments, mentions, reactions, receipts, loaded history pages) with older 
data. | Must | AC-016, AC-048 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:119:| FR-027 | The client records metric events — send latency 
(press → server confirmed), outbox wait time, send outcomes by reason, Realtime stream drops/recoveries and outage 
duration, polling activations and duration — in a bounded buffer (oldest dropped when full) and sends them 
fire-and-forget in batches of at most 50 events and 16 KB, at most one delivery in flight, each aborted after 5 s, 
dropping batches under overload. The authenticated, per-user rate-limited route rejects oversized requests, accepts 
only closed event/reason enums and bounded numeric fields, drops anything else, adds a server-derived app version, and 
writes structured log lines under BR-013. | Must | AC-028 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:136:| BR-005 | Outcome classes: transient = browser offline before 
dispatch, HTTP 429, HTTP 401 while the client session is valid → retry automatically; unknown = network 
error/timeout/no response after dispatch, lease expiry, HTTP 5xx → fence (FR-006); session-ended = client auth reports 
signed out / refresh rejected → hold (FR-012); definitive = 403 `NOT_PARTICIPANT` (incl. a missing conversation), 400 
(e.g. `INVALID_REPLY_TARGET`), 413 `CONTENT_TOO_LARGE`, 409 `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`, sender mismatch, and any other 
4xx except 401/429 → failed, no automatic retry; a stale-generation rejection only means a newer attempt exists and is 
ignored (FR-011). | R1-q5 C, R2-q4 A, R2-q5 A; reviews r2 #2, #11 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:141:| BR-010 | Mentions: only current members of the conversation 
at first creation, other than the sender, can be mentioned (rooms: members of the room conversation); no 
`@todos`/`@here`. | R1-q10 A, E; R2-q10 A; D4 |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:143:| BR-012 | Polling runs only while a required Realtime stream 
has been unhealthy for more than 30 s and the tab is visible, every 15 s (immediately on becoming visible), and no new 
poll starts once all streams are healthy. | R1-q7 A, R1-q16 D, R2-q12 C |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:147:| BR-016 | Conversation restore is per browser and per user; a 
different user never inherits it. | R2-q11 A, B, D |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:153:- Browser connectivity events, Realtime stream status, tab 
visibility, Supabase auth state changes.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:179:- Timing parameters chosen under the owner's delegation (D4) 
and adjustable in planning only with owner approval: claim request timeout 30 s, lease 45 s, metrics batch ≤ 50 events 
/ 16 KB, one metrics delivery in flight, 5 s metrics abort.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:210:| Only the conversation-list stream fails | Banner after 5 s 
and polling after 30 s follow its health; recovered independently (AC-015, AC-016). |
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:236:- UI copy in Brazilian Portuguese. The banner text, sign-out 
dialog text, typing phrases, and mention notification title in this SPEC are required; item-state labels ("pendente", 
"enviando", "expirada"), actions ("Cancelar", "Tentar de novo", "Apagar"), are proposed (the storage message in FR-033 
is the owner's D5 wording), and equivalent Portuguese wording with the same meaning is accepted (D4).
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:257:- AC-014 (FR-014): Given the drawer is open, when the browser 
goes offline, then "Sem conexão — mensagens serão enviadas quando voltar" appears immediately; when only Realtime 
drops for 3 s, no banner appears; when the messages stream alone, and separately the conversation-list stream alone, 
stays unhealthy for more than 5 s, "Reconectando…" appears; when all recover, the banner disappears without user 
action.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:258:- AC-015 (FR-015): Given each required stream is dropped 
independently (messages stream only; conversation-list stream only, including a terminal closure; typing stream only), 
when Realtime becomes available again, then that stream recovers without user action; B's messages sent during the 
outage appear once; unread counts are correct; typing works again; recorded recovery events show increasing, capped 
retry delays.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:259:- AC-016 (FR-016, BR-012; must-not R1-q16 D): Given Realtime 
is blocked while HTTP works and A's tab is visible, when B sends a message at the outage start, then A sees it within 
45 s; a message B sends after polling started appears within 15 s plus request time; the list's unread counts update; 
when C reads part of A's earlier messages during the outage (no new message sent), A's receipt summaries and open 
reader-details list update within 15 s plus request time once polling is active. Given only the conversation-list 
stream is unhealthy for more than 30 s, polling starts too. When A's tab is hidden, no polling requests are made; when 
it becomes visible, a fetch happens immediately. When Realtime recovers while a poll is in flight, the message 
delivered by both appears once, and no new polling request starts during the next 60 s.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:271:- AC-028 (FR-027, BR-013): Given a send, a stream recovery, 
and a polling episode occur, then the metrics route writes structured lines with enum event type/reason, durations, 
and server-derived version; a scan finds no message text, names, emails, or user/conversation IDs; a payload with 
unknown fields, free-text strings, out-of-range numbers, or a forged version produces no such values in the logs; a 
request over 50 events or 16 KB is rejected; requests beyond the per-user rate limit are rejected; an unauthenticated 
request is rejected and produces no metric log line.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:299:  - E2E (Playwright, real UI; two or more real accounts; two 
tabs of one account; a second device as a separate browser context): every other AC. Network shaping only — the app, 
routes, and Supabase stay real: browser-context offline mode; blocking only the Realtime WebSocket or a single stream; 
holding a request or response by delayed pass-through to the real server; dropping a response or replacing it with a 
gateway 5xx after pass-through; disabling IndexedDB or failing its writes in the page (AC-039). Old-build 
compatibility: the hosted and Phase 4 builds are run locally against the migrated database (AC-034). Expiry may use a 
test-controlled clock without changing the product rule.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/SPEC.md:305:- Mandatory failure-path tests: every AC-003..AC-013 and 
AC-038..AC-050 scenario, short vs long and per-stream Realtime drops, hidden-tab polling, polling/Realtime overlap and 
stale polls, typing tab close and conversation switch, typing access by anon/outsider/removed member, 
forged/plain/changed mentions, visible+hidden tab notification race, delayed notification authorization, 
malicious/oversized/hanging/sustained metrics traffic, restore with lost access.

